r  sorrow and err have withdrawn out of the  process so there's only one runner left  in the National Broadband Plan which is  e net doesn't really make for a good  competitive landscape with just one  player left in there so I think a  serious rethink is needed on that front  that said you know I think what we're  seeing in in Ireland is really being  heavily in  why what's happening across Europe in  general you know some of the other big  highlights that we saw on the panorama  Spain has now got to the point here  where we're hosting the conference this  year where they passed 17 and a half  million homes they've gone from zero to  hero in like four or five years they've  got over six million homes connected now  at this point so the momentum amongst  the customer base is really starting to  build as well with demand for these  services and I'm very optimistic we're  going to see this replicated across  Europe very quickly but growth also  causes a lot of demand how are they our  the industry going to cope with this it  does present a set of challenges you  know quite often when we talk about  countries like Portugal and Spain we  tend to talk about them you know they're  champions of fibre at home and we talk  about them in the past hence like  they're finished they've done if they  finish the build and it's move on but  they haven't there they're actually  still very much in full swing and  building out connectivity as we speak  and they're running our record run right  so if we look at the French market for  example they're connecting a million  homes a year so if you take that where  we've got the French market we've got  the empty Portuguese market we've got  the Spanish market all building out in  parallel we've now just seen with the UK  market and the last 12 months alone in  the UK market we've seen over two  billion pounds of investment money being  announced four different operators in  the UK market to go build air  fiber-to-the-home  networks so well it's fantastic that  we're seeing all this growth and  investment the challenges around supply  and supply not just on a raw material  side of things but also supply of human  resources to skilled people that can  design the networks that can build out  the networks the splicers that can do  the splicing work so we are starting to  hear a few grumblings out there that  there's a few pinch points emerging so I  know from speaking with the vendors here  at the show that a lot of them are  making the necessary investments to  ensure that we don't run into pinch  points as we go forward that there will  be enough supply of materials I know a  lot of them are investing heavily in  training of their personnel as well so  that the sufficient resources to keep  things going forward  and are you seeing any evidence of  innovation coming into the market or  without question there's a number of the  new entrants coming into the market that  are building out their infrastructures  that are adopting new methodologies with  regard to network design and build out  but also as well they're using new  technologies to light the fiber so for  the first time this year at one of the  operators we've seen in the UK market  now has started to deploy what we call  XJS palm technology so this is the first  of the 10 gig palm deployments now so  really what we're starting to see where  G pond technologies have served as well  for the last decade or so we're now  because the consumer demand starting to  see operators transition over onto the  10 gig palm technologies in a similar  fashion we're seeing technologies like  ng pond 2 which can take us all the way  up to 80 gig per pond with 8 different  wavelengths starting to see adoption as  well by some of the larger operators  around the world particularly those that  have mixed business models where they've  got a mix of consumer they've got a mix  of business and enterprise plus they've  also got backhaul requirements for their  mobile networks ng pond 2 really lends  itself well to that that's kind of a  hotbed of innovation at the moment  because there's a huge focus I'm trying  to drive cost out of that part of the  industry and a lot of success has been  made in 2017 I expect as we go forward  in 2080 and we really get that much more  relevant for the industry yes it looks  like we've got another dynamic 12 months  ahead in the meantime thank you for  talking to us no question I think you  know this industry never fails to  surprise me with the level of innovation  that goes on  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

